      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "This would also be useful to drive blendshapes for the eyelids using VRC's native eye tracking, so that the parts around the eye move a little when the eyes move. This can technically be done by using VRCFT's parameters, but that costs 24 bits.\n\nI just set up a system that uses 3 physbones and 2 colliders to measure the eye rotations, and drive the FT parameters for those blendshapes myself, instead of controlling them using OSC. This wouldn't be needed if I could simply use the eye muscle values to drive the blendshapes instead.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2023-08-22T21:34:51.466Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "For the purposes of driving effects based on a user's motion (for example, magic system using arm rotation, or corrective bones/blendshapes), it would be great if we could add local parameters to the FX controller to receiver muscle data.\n\nThis could be handled similarly to existing read-only animator parameters such as Viseme and Velocity. To avoid sending uncesasary values, only the parameters listed in the animator  would need to have their values synced.\n\nAdding this feature would remove  the need for complex contact, constraint, or physbone based setups to achieve the same result. For example, if I wanted to add bicep flexing to my avatar, I would either need to add a bone to each arm, and rotation constraints for each one. Alternatively, contact sender/receiver pairs could be used to drive blendshapes or bone animations for more complex motions, but now that takes up 4 contacts.",
